    <node><name>1</name><description>Stationary at both</description><answertest>AlgEquiv</answertest><sans>[vp,vq]</sans><tans>[0,0]</tans><testoptions></testoptions><quiet>1</quiet><truescoremode>=</truescoremode><truescore>0.0000000</truescore><truepenalty></truepenalty><truenextnode>2</truenextnode><trueanswernote>prt1-2-T</trueanswernote><truefeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[]]></text></truefeedback><falsescoremode>=</falsescoremode><falsescore>0.0000000</falsescore><falsepenalty></falsepenalty><falsenextnode>3</falsenextnode><falseanswernote>prt1-2-F</falseanswernote><falsefeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[]]></text></falsefeedback></node>
    <node><name>2</name><description>Maximum first</description><answertest>AlgEquiv</answertest><sans>is(w2&lt;0)</sans><tans>true</tans><testoptions></testoptions><quiet>1</quiet><truescoremode>=</truescoremode><truescore>1.0000000</truescore><truepenalty></truepenalty><truenextnode>-1</truenextnode><trueanswernote>prt1-3-T</trueanswernote><truefeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[]]></text></truefeedback><falsescoremode>=</falsescoremode><falsescore>0.0000000</falsescore><falsepenalty></falsepenalty><falsenextnode>-1</falsenextnode><falseanswernote>prt1-3-F</falseanswernote><falsefeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Your curve has stationary points at \(x = {@p0@}\) and \(x = {@q0@}\), but for your curve \(\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2}\) is \({@w2@}\) at \(x = {@p0@}\). That is positive, so your curve has a minimum point there and its maximum point at \(x = {@q0@}\). What change to your expression would turn the curve upside down?</p>]]></text></falsefeedback></node>
    <node><name>3</name><description>Roots instead</description><answertest>AlgEquiv</answertest><sans>[zp,zq]</sans><tans>[0,0]</tans><testoptions></testoptions><quiet>1</quiet><truescoremode>=</truescoremode><truescore>0.0000000</truescore><truepenalty></truepenalty><truenextnode>-1</truenextnode><trueanswernote>prt1-4-T</trueanswernote><truefeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Your curve meets the \(x\)-axis at \(x = {@p0@}\) and \(x = {@q0@}\): that is where \(y = 0\). Stationary points are where \(\dfrac{dy}{dx} = 0\), and for your curve \(\dfrac{dy}{dx}\) is \({@vp@}\) at \(x = {@p0@}\) and \({@vq@}\) at \(x = {@q0@}\). Which expression should be zero at these two values of \(x\)?</p>]]></text></truefeedback><falsescoremode>=</falsescoremode><falsescore>0.0000000</falsescore><falsepenalty></falsepenalty><falsenextnode>-1</falsenextnode><falseanswernote>prt1-4-F</falseanswernote><falsefeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>For your curve, \(\dfrac{dy}{dx}\) is \({@vp@}\) at \(x = {@p0@}\) and \({@vq@}\) at \(x = {@q0@}\). Both must be \(0\) for the curve to be stationary there. Decide which factors \(\dfrac{dy}{dx}\) must have, then find a \(y\) with that gradient.</p>]]></text></falsefeedback></node>
